## What is set today (seen read-only in the Conductor's own tab, closed after)
- **Settings → Plugins:** "Default permission: Allow read-only tools". The other menu options are "Always ask" ("Ask before reading or making changes") and "Allow low-risk tools" ("Automatically approve low-risk tools, but sensitive tools may be denied").
- **Composio's per-plugin setting** for the alex@increaseroas.com connection (Settings → Plugins → Composio → Permission) offers four choices:
  - Always ask;
  - Allow read-only tools (marked Default, selected);
  - Allow low-risk tools;

  - the cards are "model-driven and make decisions based on the overall context of the conversation";
  - read-only is no automatic exemption.
  - **What it means here:** the default prompting is a judgment call by a model, not a fixed per-call rule. That fits spp-c16, where one card was followed by silent writes.
- **"`ui/notifications/tool-input` isn't sent unless the Plugin's Permissions is set to 'Always ask'"** (2026-09-17), https://community.openai.com/t/ui-notifications-tool-input-isnt-sent-unless-the-plugins-permissions-is-set-to-always-ask/1398347. A developer reports that ChatGPT runs plugin calls differently under "Always ask" than under the read or low-risk modes. Indirect support that "Always ask" takes a stricter path. No answer yet.
- **Older reports show the behavior moving over time:**
  - every MCP call needing Confirm/Deny in developer mode, https://community.openai.com/t/feature-request-always-allow-option-for-mcp-connectors-remove-constant-confirm-deny/1359246 (2025-09);

- **Custom routes: yes in the API, experimental.** Composio API reference, "Create a new connected account" v3.1 (https://docs.composio.dev/reference/api-reference/connected-accounts/postConnectedAccounts, read 2026-10-01):
  - the same request that carries `state.authScheme` (OAUTH1, OAUTH2, API_KEY, …) takes `experimental.account_type: "PRIVATE" | "SHARED"` and `acl_config_for_shared` (`allow_all_users`, `allowed_user_ids`, `not_allowed_user_ids`);
  - so an API-key login like our key mover's can be created as SHARED.
- **The catch, from the docs.** "Shared connections" (https://docs.composio.dev/docs/shared-connections) says:
  - a SHARED connection "is never resolved implicitly", so a session reaches it only when pinned by `ca_` ID;
  - access is deny-by-default and governed by the access list, which only the creator or an API-key caller can change;
  - at most one SHARED connection per toolkit per session.

  - choose member-owned or workspace-owned per connection;
  - give workspace-owned logins a dedicated integration identity so they survive membership changes;
  - "Shared connections are … experimental … Pin and test the SDK version before making shared connections part of a production tenant model";
  - test that "a removed member can no longer invoke workspace-owned connections".

